==== Saviolo: _His Practice_ (#cite(<Saviolo_1595>, form: "year"))

#index-start[Saviolo, Vincentio]
In _His Practice_ (#cite(<Saviolo_1595>, form: "year")), Vincentio Saviolo describes three guards ('wards') for single rapier.
Although he writes that all three are useful in practice, he also emphasises the didactic quality of the first two: the first ward teaches nimbleness, judgement, and coordination; the second ward teaches the use of the empty hand, stoccata, and imbroccata.

'frame your hand, your foote, and your body, all which partes must goe together, and unlesse you can stirre and move all these together, you shall never be able to performe any great matter, but with great danger' #cite(<Saviolo_1595>, supplement: "8").

===== First Ward

#index-start("Saviolo, Vincentio", "First ward")

'breeds a judgement of the time, and a readinesse and nimblenesse as well of the hand as the foote, together with the body: and from this you come prepared to learne other wards with more facilitie, and to have a greater insight and understanding in many things' #cite(<Saviolo_1595>, supplement: "16").
On his account, 'many Nations use this ward in fight verie much, especially with the single rapier, both Italians, French-men, Spaniards, & Almanes' #cite(<Saviolo_1595>, supplement: "16").


Second, it starts from his 'second ward'#index("Guard", "Saviolo's first ward") which he describes as follows, somewhat contrary to the accompanying illustration (@saviolo_w1):

#set quote(block: true)
#quote(attribution: [#cite(
  <Saviolo_1595>,
  supplement: "8–9",
  form: "prose",
)])[[The scholler must] stand with his right foote formost, with his knee somewhat bowing, but that his body rest more upon the lefte legge, not stedfast and firme as some stand, which seeme to be nayled to the place, but with a readines and nimblenes, as though he were to perform some feate of activitie ... his [weapon] hand be free and at libertie from his body, and that the ward of his hand be directlye against his right knee ... [and] the pobnt (_sic_) be directly against the face of his [opponent].]

